## Available Official WordPress Skills

| Skill Name | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- |
| `wp-playground` | Spins up local WordPress instances, tests PHP/WP versions, and runs Blueprints. |
| `wp-block-development` | Assists with custom Gutenberg block creation, `block.json`, and React/PHP rendering. |
| `wp-block-themes` | Guides Full Site Editing (FSE), `theme.json` configuration, templates, and patterns. |
| `wp-plugin-development` | Enforces WordPress plugin lifecycle, security, sanitization, and directory standards. |
| `wp-wpcli-and-ops` | Automates site management, database operations, and maintenance via WP-CLI. |
| `wp-performance` | Assists with backend profiling, caching strategies, and database query optimization. |

---

## Usage in Codex

Once installed, reference the skill naturally in your Codex prompts:

* *"Use the `wp-playground` skill to spin up a clean WordPress 6.7 instance for testing."*
* *"Audit this custom plugin for security and coding standards using `wp-plugin-development`."*
* *"Help me configure typography presets in `theme.json` using `wp-block-themes`."*

---

## Managing Live WordPress Sites (Optional)

The official `agent-skills` repository provides local development and coding workflows. If you want Codex to connect directly to a **live WordPress site** to publish posts, edit pages, or manage media, install a WordPress Model Context Protocol (MCP) connector plugin (such as Novamira or Respira) and register its MCP server endpoint in your Codex configuration.

